[{"query":"intermediate Microsoft Excel skills formulas VLOOKUP XLOOKUP pivot tables","method":"BM25 keyword search","results":[{"chunk_id":"POL-EQUIV","source":"Melbourne Business Services - Recruitment and Selection Policy (fictional)","section":"4.2 Qualifications and "or equivalent"","title":"Equivalent qualifications and experience, and which payroll systems count","score":11.88,"text":"4.2.1 Where a criterion says "or equivalent", it is met by:
- a higher qualification in the same field, for example a Diploma or Bachelor degree in accounting, finance or commerce, or a CPA or CA qualification; or
- at least three years of payroll or accounts processing experience, which counts as equivalent to the Certificate IV.

4.2.2 Qualifications are judged on their content and level, not on the name or prestige of the institution.

4.2.3 For desirable criterion D1 (payroll software), the systems named in the advertisement are examples only. Experience with any recognised payroll system meets D1, including Employment Hero (formerly KeyPay), ADP, SAP, Ascender and Micropay. Experience with more than one system, or with administering or implementing a system, may score 3.

4.2.4 For E3 (Microsoft Excel), the applicant must show the named functions, or equivalent ones such as INDEX/MATCH in place of VLOOKUP, or work that clearly requires them, such as building reconciliation spreadsheets."},{"chunk_id":"POL-GAPS","source":"Melbourne Business Services - Recruitment and Selection Policy (fictional)","section":"4.3 Career breaks and gaps in employment","title":"Career breaks must not be scored negatively","score":2.87,"text":"4.3.1 A career break or gap in employment, of any length and for any reason or none, must not lower any score and must not be mentioned as a weakness.

4.3.2 Applicants do not have to explain a gap. Assessors must not guess at the reason, such as parenting, illness or age, because such guesses relate to protected attributes (parental or carer status, disability, age).

4.3.3 Experience gained before a career break counts in full towards the experience criteria. None of the MBS Payroll Officer criteria require recent experience. Assessors must therefore not add a recency requirement that is not in the advertisement.

4.3.4 If the assessor believes that knowledge may have changed during a break, for example award rates or payroll legislation, this is recorded as a question for interview. It is never a deduction at screening.

4.3.5 Basis: indirect discrimination provisions of the Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth) and the Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic). Career breaks are taken disproportionately by women and carers."},{"chunk_id":"POL-IRRELEVANT","source":"Melbourne Business Services - Recruitment and Selection Policy (fictional)","section":"6 Information that must not influence screening","title":"Personal details that must be ignored and not repeated","score":1.87,"text":"6.1 The following must not influence any score and must not be repeated in assessment notes:
- name;
- photo;
- date of birth or age;
- gender or pronouns;
- marital or relationship status;
- children or pregnancy;
- religion, or religious or community affiliations;
- nationality or ethnicity;
- home address or suburb;
- hobbies and interests not related to the criteria.

6.2 Volunteer or community work is assessed only where it gives evidence for a selection criterion, for example volunteer treasurer duties as evidence of reconciliation skills. The organisation's religious, cultural or political nature is ignored.

6.3 Work rights (visa or residency) are confirmed by the Recruiter at offer stage and are not scored at screening."},{"chunk_id":"POL-SCORING","source":"Melbourne Business Services - Recruitment and Selection Policy (fictional)","section":"3.1-3.4 Scoring against selection criteria","title":"Scoring scale, evidence rule and shortlisting threshold","score":1.82,"text":"3.1 Each selection criterion is scored separately, using only evidence in the application:
- 0 = no evidence;
- 1 = partial evidence (related but incomplete, or the required level is not shown);
- 2 = meets the criterion;
- 3 = clearly exceeds the criterion.

3.2 Every score above 0 must be supported by the applicant's own words. Assessors must not assume skills that are not stated. They must also not mark down a skill because it is described briefly, if the description clearly shows the skill.

3.3 An applicant is shortlisted only if every essential criterion scores 2 or more. Desirable criteria are used only to rank applicants who meet all essential criteria. A low desirable score never removes an applicant.

3.4 Where a criterion cannot be scored confidently from the application, the assessor scores what the evidence supports, records "information missing" with the specific question to ask, and refers the application to the Recruiter. Uncertainty is never resolved against the applicant."}]}]

[{"query":"Australian Modern Awards National Employment Standards NES scoring","method":"BM25 keyword search","results":[{"chunk_id":"POL-OVERSEAS","source":"Melbourne Business Services - Recruitment and Selection Policy (fictional)","section":"4.4 Overseas qualifications and experience; names and language background","title":"Overseas qualifications, overseas experience, names and language background","score":15.81,"text":"4.4.1 Overseas qualifications and experience are assessed on whether their content and level are equivalent to the criterion, not on the country where they were gained.

4.4.2 "Australian experience" is not a criterion for the Payroll Officer role. Knowledge of Australian Modern Awards and the National Employment Standards is a separate desirable criterion (D2) and is scored only on stated evidence of that knowledge.

4.4.3 If equivalence of an overseas qualification cannot be judged from the application, the assessor records "information missing: qualification equivalence" and refers to the Recruiter. The applicant is not scored as having no qualification.

4.4.4 An applicant's name, country of birth, accent or language background must not influence any score. Written communication is assessed only if it is a stated criterion."},{"chunk_id":"EOA-ADS","source":"Equal Opportunity Act 2010 (Vic)","section":"s 182 (discriminatory advertising); VEOHRC Guideline: Recruitment ss 1.5.1, 3.5","title":"Discriminatory job advertisements and selection criteria","score":5.72,"text":"Under section 182, it is an offence to publish or display an advertisement, or to authorise one, that indicates an intention to discriminate. Penalties apply to both organisations and individuals.

The VEOHRC recruitment guideline says advertisements should focus on the skills and abilities the job requires. It warns that phrases such as "young, dynamic team" or "mature, experienced professional" signal an age preference and should be avoided.

When writing selection criteria, the guideline advises employers to:
- clarify the genuine requirements of the job first;
- avoid discriminatory language and irrelevant personal attributes;
- separate essential from desirable criteria and rank them by importance.

Examples of problem criteria in a payroll role:
- "native English speaker": race or national origin proxy;
- "young and energetic": age proxy;
- "recent graduate": age proxy;
- "must have Australian experience": national origin proxy, unless genuinely required;
- "physically fit": disability proxy, unless an inherent requirement with a stated reason.

Such criteria should be removed from scoring and referred to HR to amend the advertisement."},{"chunk_id":"RDA-15","source":"Racial Discrimination Act 1975 (Cth)","section":"s 15; s 9 (racial discrimination); Australian Human Rights Commission employer guidance","title":"Racial discrimination in hiring - race, colour, descent, national or ethnic origin, immigrant status","score":4.88,"text":"Section 15 of the Racial Discrimination Act makes it unlawful for an employer to refuse or fail to employ a person, or to offer a person less favourable terms, by reason of the person's race, colour, or national or ethnic origin.

The Australian Human Rights Commission's employer guidance says discrimination because of race, colour, descent, national or ethnic origin or immigrant status is unlawful "during recruitment and selection processes". It gives these examples of unlawful conduct:
- refusing to employ someone because of a generalisation about their racial group;
- not employing someone because of assumptions that they "wouldn't 'fit in'".

Two limits apply. The Act does not prohibit discrimination based solely on nationality or citizenship, and an employer is not required to employ someone without a valid work visa. Work rights are therefore a separate, lawful question for a recruiter to confirm.

Practical meaning for screening: a candidate's name, country of birth or language background must not affect any score. Identical resumes with different names should receive the same scores."},{"chunk_id":"POL-SCORING","source":"Melbourne Business Services - Recruitment and Selection Policy (fictional)","section":"3.1-3.4 Scoring against selection criteria","title":"Scoring scale, evidence rule and shortlisting threshold","score":3.82,"text":"3.1 Each selection criterion is scored separately, using only evidence in the application:
